<div class="calcpad-output">
 <h3>Floor Load Takedown</h3>
 <p>
  A worked takedown that exercises the parts of
  <b>#UI</b>
  built for input forms: grids seeded
  <p>from an expression, units carried through a grid of plain numbers, the width properties, and the</p>
  <p>two properties that trade the guard rails for flexibility. Switch to Input mode to fill it in.</p>
 </p>
 <p>
 </p>
 <h3>1. A grid seeded from an expression</h3>
 <p>
 </p>
 <p>
  A datagrid replaces its whole right hand side the moment it is edited, so it can be seeded
  <p>with any expression at all - not only a literal. That is what lets a blank schedule start at</p>
  <p>something more useful than zeros; every cell below starts at 5 kN/m².</p>
 </p>
 <p>
 </p>
 <p>
  The cells hold plain numbers and the unit is kept aside - hover a cell to see it. It goes
  <p>back on when the grid writes its literal, so nothing downstream has to guess. Where the unit</p>
  <p>matters at a glance it belongs in the header, and a header may contain a comma.</p>
 </p>
 <p>
 </p>
 <p>
  <span class="eq">
   <b>
    <var>w</var>
   </b>
   =
   <b>matrix</b>
   ( 4; 2 )  + 5
   <span class="dvc">
    <i>kN</i>
    <span class="dvl">
    </span>
    <i>m</i>
    <sup class="unit">2</sup>
   </span>
   =
   <span class="matrix">
    <span class="tr">
     <span class="td">
     </span>
     <span class="td">
      5
      <i>kN</i>
      <i class="unit">∕</i>
      <i>m</i>
      <sup class="unit">2</sup>
     </span>
     <span class="td">
      5
      <i>kN</i>
      <i class="unit">∕</i>
      <i>m</i>
      <sup class="unit">2</sup>
     </span>
     <span class="td">
     </span>
    </span>
    <span class="tr">
     <span class="td">
     </span>
     <span class="td">
      5
      <i>kN</i>
      <i class="unit">∕</i>
      <i>m</i>
      <sup class="unit">2</sup>
     </span>
     <span class="td">
      5
      <i>kN</i>
      <i class="unit">∕</i>
      <i>m</i>
      <sup class="unit">2</sup>
     </span>
     <span class="td">
     </span>
    </span>
    <span class="tr">
     <span class="td">
     </span>
     <span class="td">
      5
      <i>kN</i>
      <i class="unit">∕</i>
      <i>m</i>
      <sup class="unit">2</sup>
     </span>
     <span class="td">
      5
      <i>kN</i>
      <i class="unit">∕</i>
      <i>m</i>
      <sup class="unit">2</sup>
     </span>
     <span class="td">
     </span>
    </span>
    <span class="tr">
     <span class="td">
     </span>
     <span class="td">
      5
      <i>kN</i>
      <i class="unit">∕</i>
      <i>m</i>
      <sup class="unit">2</sup>
     </span>
     <span class="td">
      5
      <i>kN</i>
      <i class="unit">∕</i>
      <i>m</i>
      <sup class="unit">2</sup>
     </span>
     <span class="td">
     </span>
    </span>
   </span>
  </span>
 </p>
 <p>
 </p>
 <h3>2. Sizing</h3>
 <p>
 </p>
 <p>
  A grid takes its natural width - the row header plus 80 px a column - unless
  <b>width</b>
  says otherwise. Above it is
  <b>"100%"</b>
  , with
  <b>columnWidths</b>
  read as ratios
  <p>and scaled to fit. Here the same shape is left to size itself, then pinned to 420 px.</p>
 </p>
 <p>
 </p>
 <p>
  <span class="eq">
   <b>
    <var>w</var>
   </b>
   <sub>nat</sub>
   =
   <b>matrix</b>
   ( 4; 2 )  =
   <span class="matrix">
    <span class="tr">
     <span class="td">
     </span>
     <span class="td">0</span>
     <span class="td">0</span>
     <span class="td">
     </span>
    </span>
    <span class="tr">
     <span class="td">
     </span>
     <span class="td">0</span>
     <span class="td">0</span>
     <span class="td">
     </span>
    </span>
    <span class="tr">
     <span class="td">
     </span>
     <span class="td">0</span>
     <span class="td">0</span>
     <span class="td">
     </span>
    </span>
    <span class="tr">
     <span class="td">
     </span>
     <span class="td">0</span>
     <span class="td">0</span>
     <span class="td">
     </span>
    </span>
   </span>
  </span>
 </p>
 <p>
  <span class="eq">
   <b>
    <var>w</var>
   </b>
   <sub>fix</sub>
   =
   <b>matrix</b>
   ( 4; 2 )  =
   <span class="matrix">
    <span class="tr">
     <span class="td">
     </span>
     <span class="td">0</span>
     <span class="td">0</span>
     <span class="td">
     </span>
    </span>
    <span class="tr">
     <span class="td">
     </span>
     <span class="td">0</span>
     <span class="td">0</span>
     <span class="td">
     </span>
    </span>
    <span class="tr">
     <span class="td">
     </span>
     <span class="td">0</span>
     <span class="td">0</span>
     <span class="td">
     </span>
    </span>
    <span class="tr">
     <span class="td">
     </span>
     <span class="td">0</span>
     <span class="td">0</span>
     <span class="td">
     </span>
    </span>
   </span>
  </span>
 </p>
 <p>
 </p>
 <p>
  A grid of a single column is sized from its header rather than stretched across the page,
  <p>and a header that spills wraps instead of clipping.</p>
 </p>
 <p>
 </p>
 <p>
  <span class="eq">
   <b>
    <var>ψ</var>
   </b>
   =
   <b>matrix</b>
   ( 4; 1 )  + 1 =
   <span class="matrix">
    <span class="tr">
     <span class="td">
     </span>
     <span class="td">1</span>
     <span class="td">
     </span>
    </span>
    <span class="tr">
     <span class="td">
     </span>
     <span class="td">1</span>
     <span class="td">
     </span>
    </span>
    <span class="tr">
     <span class="td">
     </span>
     <span class="td">1</span>
     <span class="td">
     </span>
    </span>
    <span class="tr">
     <span class="td">
     </span>
     <span class="td">1</span>
     <span class="td">
     </span>
    </span>
   </span>
  </span>
 </p>
 <p>
 </p>
 <h5>Long names that should wrap</h5>
 <p>
  <span class="eq">
   <b>
    <var>longNames</var>
   </b>
   =
   <b>matrix</b>
   ( 4; 1 )  + 1 =
   <span class="matrix">
    <span class="tr">
     <span class="td">
     </span>
     <span class="td">1</span>
     <span class="td">
     </span>
    </span>
    <span class="tr">
     <span class="td">
     </span>
     <span class="td">1</span>
     <span class="td">
     </span>
    </span>
    <span class="tr">
     <span class="td">
     </span>
     <span class="td">1</span>
     <span class="td">
     </span>
    </span>
    <span class="tr">
     <span class="td">
     </span>
     <span class="td">1</span>
     <span class="td">
     </span>
    </span>
   </span>
  </span>
 </p>
 <p>
 </p>
 <h3>3. Units typed into the cells</h3>
 <p>
 </p>
 <p>
  With
  <b>forceUnits</b>
  off the units move into the cells, so each one carries its own -
  <p>which is what a schedule needs when it mixes point loads with pressures. Type</p>
  <b>2kN</b>
  ,
  <b>0.5MN</b>
  , whatever suits the line.
 </p>
 <p>
 </p>
 <p>
  <span class="eq">
   <b>
    <var>P</var>
   </b>
   =
   <b>matrix</b>
   ( 3; 1 )  + 2
   <i>kN</i>
   =
   <span class="matrix">
    <span class="tr">
     <span class="td">
     </span>
     <span class="td">
      2
      <i>kN</i>
     </span>
     <span class="td">
     </span>
    </span>
    <span class="tr">
     <span class="td">
     </span>
     <span class="td">
      2
      <i>kN</i>
     </span>
     <span class="td">
     </span>
    </span>
    <span class="tr">
     <span class="td">
     </span>
     <span class="td">
      2
      <i>kN</i>
     </span>
     <span class="td">
     </span>
    </span>
   </span>
  </span>
 </p>
 <p>
 </p>
 <h3>4. Expressions in the cells</h3>
 <p>
 </p>
 <p>
  With
  <b>allowExpression</b>
  a cell takes anything and it is written to the document as typed,
  <p>
   so a tributary width can be entered as
   <b>6m/2</b>
   and stay that way, rather than collapsing to
  </p>
  <p>a number the next reader has to reverse-engineer. Nothing is checked as you type: an entry the</p>
  <p>parser cannot read leaves the control in place and reports the error on the line below it.</p>
 </p>
 <p>
 </p>
 <p>
  <span class="eq">
   <b>
    <var>b</var>
   </b>
   =
   <b>matrix</b>
   ( 4; 1 )  +
   <span class="dvc">
    6
    <i>m</i>
    <span class="dvl">
    </span>
    2
   </span>
   =
   <span class="matrix">
    <span class="tr">
     <span class="td">
     </span>
     <span class="td">
      3
      <i>m</i>
     </span>
     <span class="td">
     </span>
    </span>
    <span class="tr">
     <span class="td">
     </span>
     <span class="td">
      3
      <i>m</i>
     </span>
     <span class="td">
     </span>
    </span>
    <span class="tr">
     <span class="td">
     </span>
     <span class="td">
      3
      <i>m</i>
     </span>
     <span class="td">
     </span>
    </span>
    <span class="tr">
     <span class="td">
     </span>
     <span class="td">
      3
      <i>m</i>
     </span>
     <span class="td">
     </span>
    </span>
   </span>
  </span>
 </p>
 <p>
 </p>
 <h3>5. The same two properties on an entry</h3>
 <p>
 </p>
 <p>By default an entry edits the number and the unit stays in the document beside the box.</p>
 <p>
 </p>
 <p>
  <span class="eq">
   <var>L</var>
   = 6
   <i>m</i>
  </span>
 </p>
 <p>
 </p>
 <p>
  With
  <b>forceUnits</b>
  off the unit moves into the box, so the input can change its unit as
  <p>
   well as its magnitude - enter
   <b>250mm</b>
   or
   <b>0.25m</b>
   , whichever reads better.
  </p>
 </p>
 <p>
 </p>
 <p>
  <span class="eq">
   <var>t</var>
   = 200
   <i>mm</i>
  </span>
 </p>
 <p>
 </p>
 <p>
  With
  <b>allowExpression</b>
  the box shows the expression as written rather than the number it
  <p>came to, so a factor built from its parts can be adjusted instead of flattened on first touch.</p>
 </p>
 <p>
 </p>
 <p>
  <span class="eq">
   <var>γ</var>
   <sub>G</sub>
   = 1.25 + 0.1 = 1.35
  </span>
 </p>
 <p>
 </p>
 <h3>6. The takedown</h3>
 <p>
 </p>
 <p>
  <span class="eq">
   <var>g</var>
   <sub>k</sub>
   =
   <b>sum</b>
   (
   <b>col</b>
   (
   <b>
    <var>w</var>
   </b>
   ; 1 )  ·
   <b>col</b>
   (
   <b>
    <var>b</var>
   </b>
   ; 1 )  )  = 60
   <i>kN</i>
   <i class="unit">∕</i>
   <i>m</i>
  </span>
 </p>
 <p>
  <span class="eq">
   <var>q</var>
   <sub>k</sub>
   =
   <b>sum</b>
   (
   <b>col</b>
   (
   <b>
    <var>w</var>
   </b>
   ; 2 )  ·
   <b>col</b>
   (
   <b>
    <var>b</var>
   </b>
   ; 1 )  ·
   <b>col</b>
   (
   <b>
    <var>ψ</var>
   </b>
   ; 1 )  )  = 60
   <i>kN</i>
   <i class="unit">∕</i>
   <i>m</i>
  </span>
 </p>
 <p>
  <span class="eq">
   <var>G</var>
   <sub>k</sub>
   =
   <var>g</var>
   <sub>k</sub>
   ·
   <var>L</var>
   +
   <b>sum</b>
   (
   <b>
    <var>P</var>
   </b>
   )  = 60
   <i>kN</i>
   <i class="unit">∕</i>
   <i>m</i>
   · 6
   <i>m</i>
   +
   <b>sum</b>
   (
   <b>
    <var>P</var>
   </b>
   )  = 366
   <i>kN</i>
  </span>
 </p>
 <p>
  <span class="eq">
   <var>Q</var>
   <sub>k</sub>
   =
   <var>q</var>
   <sub>k</sub>
   ·
   <var>L</var>
   = 60
   <i>kN</i>
   <i class="unit">∕</i>
   <i>m</i>
   · 6
   <i>m</i>
   = 360
   <i>kN</i>
  </span>
 </p>
 <p>
  <span class="eq">
   <var>F</var>
   <sub>d</sub>
   =
   <var>γ</var>
   <sub>G</sub>
   ·
   <var>G</var>
   <sub>k</sub>
   + 1.5 ·
   <var>Q</var>
   <sub>k</sub>
   = 1.35 · 366
   <i>kN</i>
   + 1.5 · 360
   <i>kN</i>
   = 1034.1
   <i>kN</i>
  </span>
 </p>
 <p>
 </p>
 <p>
  Design load carried into the column below, over a floor plate
  <b>L</b>
  long with a
  <b>t</b>
  slab.
 </p>
</div>
